# MASTER BUILD PROMPT — INTERNFLOW
## Full-Stack Internship Management System | Software Engineering Project

Act as a senior full-stack software architect, product designer, database engineer, UI/UX specialist and QA engineer.

Your task is to DESIGN, DEVELOP, INTEGRATE, TEST AND POLISH a complete, functional, production-style web application called **InternFlow — Internship Management System (IMS)**.

This is not a static website, landing-page-only project, UI prototype or collection of disconnected dashboards. Build one complete, interconnected application with a real backend, persistent database, authentication, role-based access control, document management, application workflows, notifications and analytics.

The system must follow the Software Requirements Specification (SRS) for an Internship Management System involving three user categories:

1. Students
2. Companies / Recruiters
3. College Administrators

The application must be impressive enough for a university Software Engineering project demonstration while maintaining realistic, maintainable engineering practices.

---

# 1. PRIMARY OBJECTIVE

Create a centralized internship management ecosystem where:

- Students can register, maintain their profiles, discover opportunities, upload resumes, apply for internships and track their progress.
- Companies can register, create company profiles, publish internship opportunities, review candidates, shortlist applicants, schedule interviews and update selection decisions.
- Administrators can manage users, verify companies, approve internship postings, monitor applications, track internship completion and generate reports.

Every module must communicate with the same backend and database.

A change made by one role must be reflected in the appropriate dashboards of other roles.

Example:

When a recruiter shortlists a student:
- The application's status must change in the database.
- The student must see the updated status in their dashboard.
- A notification must be generated for the student.
- The recruiter must see the updated applicant pipeline.
- The administrator must see the updated application record.

No isolated modules or hardcoded interactions.

# 2. TECHNOLOGY STACK

Use the following architecture, prioritizing fast implementation, stability and easy deployment.

Frontend:
- React
- TypeScript
- Modern component-based architecture
- Tailwind CSS
- Lucide React icons
- React Router
- Framer Motion for restrained transitions
- Recharts for analytics
- React Hook Form and Zod for forms and validation

Backend:
- Node.js server runtime
- Secure server-side API endpoints
- Firebase Authentication
- Cloud Firestore as the persistent database
- Firebase Storage for resumes and internship documents
- Firebase Security Rules for data access control

Additional libraries:
- date-fns for date handling
- Sonner or an equivalent toast notification library
- A lightweight charting library
- A PDF generation library for reports if practical

Use the Firebase integration available in the development environment. Configure the actual authentication and database connections rather than creating mock services.

Keep API calls and privileged operations on the server where appropriate. Never expose secret credentials in frontend code.

IMPORTANT DATABASE NOTE:
The initial academic SRS lists MySQL. For this rapid implementation, use Firestore because of the available integrated Firebase provisioning. Keep data models and repository operations modular so a relational MySQL implementation can be substituted later if required. Document this technology decision.

